00:11:09,390 --> 00:11:12,593
So this gives us back an object. A knapsack

270
00:11:12,883 --> 00:11:15,573
where all other items have been added or not

271
00:11:15,783 --> 00:11:18,215
based on the assumption that this item for

272
00:11:18,215 --> 00:11:19,472
this index was added.

273
00:11:20,148 --> 00:11:22,464
So therefore, the valueWithItem is the

274
00:11:22,464 --> 00:11:24,147
sackWithItem.value.

275
00:11:25,672 --> 00:11:28,476
Keep in mind that knapsack should be an

276
00:11:28,736 --> 00:11:31,313
object that has a value property eventually.

277
00:11:31,929 --> 00:11:33,307
So we'll have the sackWithItem.value,

278
00:11:34,512 --> 00:11:37,432
but we have to add the value of the item

279
00:11:37,432 --> 00:11:38,476
we're about to add.

280
00:11:39,299 --> 00:11:39,701
So here

281
00:11:40,247 --> 00:11:43,153
I also add items for the item index dot

282
00:11:43,602 --> 00:11:43,987
value.

283
00:11:44,581 --> 00:11:45,866
We already took the

284
00:11:46,412 --> 00:11:49,302
weight into account here when we reduced the

285
00:11:49,302 --> 00:11:52,111
capacity. Now we also have to take the value

286
00:11:52,192 --> 00:11:53,428
of this item into account.

287
00:11:54,391 --> 00:11:56,302
Now the value without item, of course, is a

288
00:11:56,302 --> 00:11:58,854
bit simpler. That's just the sack without

289
00:11:58,935 --> 00:12:01,487
item dot value, and we don't add anything to

290
00:12:01,503 --> 00:12:01,648
it

291
00:12:02,226 --> 00:12:04,827
because we just take that unchanged value.

292
00:12:06,093 --> 00:12:08,369
Now with these 2 values, we can add an if

293
00:12:08,481 --> 00:12:11,461
check and check if the value with item

294
00:12:12,086 --> 00:12:14,906
is greater than the value without item. So

295
00:12:14,906 --> 00:12:17,438
if the knapsack we derived without adding

296
00:12:17,791 --> 00:12:20,947
this specific item here is better or worse

297
00:12:21,059 --> 00:12:22,726
than if we did add the item.

298
00:12:23,559 --> 00:12:25,562
And value with item and value without item

299
00:12:25,562 --> 00:12:27,325
will just be a number, so we can compare

300
00:12:27,325 --> 00:12:27,854
them like this.

301
00:12:29,071 --> 00:12:31,523
Now if the value with item

302
00:12:32,117 --> 00:12:34,045
is greater than the value without item,

303
00:12:34,623 --> 00:12:37,226
we know that we wanna return

304
00:12:37,901 --> 00:12:40,922
our updated knapsack. So basically our

305
00:12:40,986 --> 00:12:43,717
knapsack that was returned here, so the sack

306
00:12:43,926 --> 00:12:44,537
with item,

307
00:12:45,147 --> 00:12:46,063
but where we also

308
00:12:46,818 --> 00:12:49,067
increment the weight and the value by this

309
00:12:49,148 --> 00:12:51,429
item's value and weight, and where we also

310
00:12:51,638 --> 00:12:53,261
add this item to the items array.

311
00:12:53,984 --> 00:12:55,398
So we can get an updated

312
00:12:56,169 --> 00:12:58,049
sack here, which is an object.

313
00:12:59,044 --> 00:13:00,986
And in there we'll have items, and that's

314
00:13:00,986 --> 00:13:01,868
our sackWithItem.items.

315
00:13:04,322 --> 00:13:05,338
Again, keep in mind,

316
00:13:05,935 --> 00:13:08,807
this sackWithItem will be an object that has

317
00:13:08,904 --> 00:13:11,098
an items property, that has a value

318
00:13:11,098 --> 00:13:13,211
property, and that has a weight property.

319
00:13:13,663 --> 00:13:15,554
That's how our knapsack should look like in

320
00:13:15,619 --> 00:13:16,346
this algorithm.

321
00:13:17,090 --> 00:13:18,868
So here I can access.items,

322
00:13:19,401 --> 00:13:21,067
but I wanna concat something there.

323
00:13:21,762 --> 00:13:23,852
And concat is a default method we can call

324
00:13:23,852 --> 00:13:26,937
on any array to create a new array by adding

325
00:13:27,306 --> 00:13:28,142
an item to the array.

326
00:13:28,994 --> 00:13:31,726
So here I then want to add items for the

327
00:13:31,726 --> 00:13:35,277
given item index. So I add this item to my

328
00:13:35,310 --> 00:13:35,585
array.

329
00:13:37,106 --> 00:13:39,727
And next, I also want to update the value.

330
00:13:39,808 --> 00:13:42,833
So I take the sack with item.value and add

331
00:13:42,930 --> 00:13:43,286
items.

332
00:13:44,095 --> 00:13:45,115
for the item index.value.

333
00:13:46,346 --> 00:13:47,983
And the same for the weight of the knapsack.

334
00:13:48,064 --> 00:13:50,950
I take my sack with item, the weight of it,

335
00:13:51,307 --> 00:13:54,889
and add items for this index.weight.

336
00:13:55,644 --> 00:13:56,768
That's the idea here.

337
00:13:57,652 --> 00:13:59,499
And then here in this case, we can return

338
00:13:59,563 --> 00:14:01,812
the updated sack just like this.

339
00:14:02,615 --> 00:14:04,286
In the else case,

340
00:14:05,683 --> 00:14:06,454
we return

341
00:14:07,032 --> 00:14:10,422
just our sack without item. So the, oops,

342
00:14:10,502 --> 00:14:12,590
without item. So the unchanged sack.

343
00:14:14,020 --> 00:14:16,172
These are different sacks we can derive.

344
00:14:16,927 --> 00:14:19,192
Now this is almost it, but we're not

345
00:14:19,192 --> 00:14:20,429
entirely there yet.

346
00:14:21,328 --> 00:14:23,625
We of course do have one if check here,

347
00:14:24,235 --> 00:14:27,319
but we always in all branches here call

348
00:14:27,528 --> 00:14:30,307
ourselves in the if check and outside of it.

349
00:14:30,580 --> 00:14:32,347
And therefore we would have an infinite

350
00:14:32,347 --> 00:14:34,804
loop. We always continue calling ourselves.

351
00:14:35,141 --> 00:14:36,828
So we need some exit condition. We need some

352
00:14:36,844 --> 00:14:37,454
base case.

353
00:14:38,209 --> 00:14:40,072
And how could the base case look like here?

354
00:14:40,682 --> 00:14:42,546
Well, I would say before we check whether

355
00:14:42,546 --> 00:14:44,891
the item should go into the knapsack, so if

356
00:14:44,955 --> 00:14:46,449
the item weight is too heavy,

357
00:14:47,106 --> 00:14:49,351
we check if we have an item because, for

358
00:14:49,351 --> 00:14:51,707
example, if the item index is smaller than

359
00:14:51,755 --> 00:14:54,625
0, we won't find the item for that index.

360
00:14:55,202 --> 00:14:57,751
And it can get smaller than 0 because we

361
00:14:57,927 --> 00:15:00,348
always reduce the item index by 1 here.

362
00:15:01,614 --> 00:15:03,891
So if the item index is smaller than 0,

363
00:15:04,596 --> 00:15:06,327
then I want to return

364
00:15:07,161 --> 00:15:10,223
a dummy knapsack, basically my base knapsack

365
00:15:10,848 --> 00:15:13,387
where I have no items and because for item

366
00:15:13,484 --> 00:15:16,326
index smaller than 0, there is nothing to

367
00:15:16,326 --> 00:15:18,781
evaluate, and where I have no value and I

368
00:15:18,894 --> 00:15:21,220
have no weight. So this here really is my

369
00:15:21,220 --> 00:15:24,005
base knapsack, which is returned under those

370
00:15:24,136 --> 00:15:25,086
circumstances.

371
00:15:27,442 --> 00:15:30,006
And we have another scenario as well where I

372
00:15:30,038 --> 00:15:32,001
want to return that, and that would be if

373
00:15:32,001 --> 00:15:33,428
the capacity is exhausted.

374
00:15:34,258 --> 00:15:37,514
Then, if the capacity is 0, I also want to

375
00:15:37,562 --> 00:15:39,400
return this base knapsack because

376
00:15:39,946 --> 00:15:42,735
There is no need for any more nested calls.

377
00:15:42,847 --> 00:15:45,573
We don't need to derive any other knapsack

378
00:15:46,037 --> 00:15:47,705
under the assumption that we're going to add

379
00:15:47,817 --> 00:15:49,580
any other item because if we have no

380
00:15:49,580 --> 00:15:50,542
capacity left,

381
00:15:51,103 --> 00:15:54,069
this knapsack will be empty. Now this can be

382
00:15:54,069 --> 00:15:56,281
the result of some deeply nested recursive

383
00:15:56,297 --> 00:15:58,638
call and therefore it's this base knapsack

384
00:15:58,878 --> 00:16:01,187
which will ultimately be returned by one of

385
00:16:01,187 --> 00:16:04,136
those other knapsack calls where then an

386
00:16:04,249 --> 00:16:05,579
item is added and so on.

387
00:16:07,870 --> 00:16:11,082
And with that, we have a function that might

388
00:16:11,179 --> 00:16:13,599
work and that might do the trick.

389
00:16:17,235 --> 00:16:19,099
So let's comment this back in here

390
00:16:19,726 --> 00:16:20,514
and let's save this

391
00:16:21,173 --> 00:16:21,864
and reload.

392
00:16:22,604 --> 00:16:24,468
And I get my items here,

393
00:16:25,897 --> 00:16:28,430
A and C, which were selected. I get a value

394
00:16:28,430 --> 00:16:30,460
of 13 and a weight of 6. And this is the

395
00:16:30,460 --> 00:16:32,457
best possible solution, as we found out on

396
00:16:32,474 --> 00:16:33,188
the slide earlier.

397
00:16:33,708 --> 00:16:36,241
So this seems to work, and we can play

398
00:16:36,241 --> 00:16:37,925
around with this. If I reduce the max

399
00:16:37,925 --> 00:16:41,020
capacity to 5, only C should be taken now.

400
00:16:41,164 --> 00:16:41,581
So if I

401
00:16:42,110 --> 00:16:42,639
reload,

402
00:16:43,377 --> 00:16:44,740
yeah, we have just C in there.

403
00:16:45,461 --> 00:16:48,412
If I increase it to 20, on the other hand,

404
00:16:48,588 --> 00:16:50,208
all items should be in the sack,

405
00:16:51,234 --> 00:16:53,655
and they are. All items are included, and

406
00:16:53,655 --> 00:16:55,691
the total value is the total value of all

407
00:16:55,884 --> 00:16:57,872
items, and the weight is the, well, weight

408
00:16:57,984 --> 00:17:00,726
of all items. So this seems to work, and

409
00:17:00,726 --> 00:17:03,197
this is one way of solving this knapsack

410
00:17:03,197 --> 00:17:03,598
problem.

411
00:17:04,786 --> 00:17:07,771
So this seems to work, and that is the

412
00:17:07,931 --> 00:17:10,402
knapsack algorithm, which by the way is a

413
00:17:10,402 --> 00:17:11,879
common interview question.

414
00:17:12,488 --> 00:17:13,901
And this hopefully showed you

415
00:17:14,494 --> 00:17:17,399
how we can approach this problem and come up

416
00:17:17,543 --> 00:17:20,640
with it. Now again, there certainly are

417
00:17:20,849 --> 00:17:22,678
elements in this solution that can be

418
00:17:22,678 --> 00:17:25,310
intimidating. Coming up with recursion and

419
00:17:25,647 --> 00:17:28,230
using that as a first step instead of a for

420
00:17:28,311 --> 00:17:30,895
loop often is unintuitive because we

421
00:17:31,424 --> 00:17:33,832
consider for loops to be more intuitive, at

422
00:17:33,848 --> 00:17:35,100
least a lot of developers do.

423
00:17:35,774 --> 00:17:38,005
But once you get the hang of recursion and

424
00:17:38,005 --> 00:17:40,204
you understand that this allows you to, for

425
00:17:40,204 --> 00:17:42,595
example, build up such a decision tree and

426
00:17:42,595 --> 00:17:45,452
that this allows you to combine values in an

427
00:17:45,548 --> 00:17:47,651
elegant way and simplify the problem by

428
00:17:47,651 --> 00:17:49,802
splitting it into subproblems, once you get

429
00:17:49,802 --> 00:17:52,193
the hang of that, you will be much more

430
00:17:52,305 --> 00:17:55,258
comfortable using recursion. And that is the

431
00:17:55,258 --> 00:17:57,199
practice and experience part I mentioned.

432
00:17:57,520 --> 00:17:59,461
You need to practice a lot to feel

433
00:17:59,589 --> 00:18:02,317
comfortable looking into such solutions so

434
00:18:02,317 --> 00:18:04,402
that this is not intimidating, but something

435
00:18:04,482 --> 00:18:06,825
you always consider as one possible

436
00:18:06,825 --> 00:18:07,242
solution.

437
00:18:07,787 --> 00:18:11,076
Now, speaking of one possible solution, this

